course: dictionary
lesson: 13093
title: A word from old American stories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one place or one time. «Fink» is old American slang, so you will mostly find it in old films, comics and stories. The clues around it tell you what it means.
explain: «The gangster yelled, "You fink! You told the police where we hid the money!"» The police and the telling are the clues. A fink is someone who tells on other people. Check it by swapping in another word, such as snitch, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «To fink on someone» means to tell on them. Calling someone a fink is a mild insult, not a very big one. Today people say snitch, grass (UK) or tattletale (US) instead.
fact: «Fink» can be a noun: a fink is a person who tells on others to the police or to people in charge.
fact: «Fink» can be a verb: to fink on someone means to tell on them, so the verb is «fink», «finks», «finked» or «finking».
fact: «Fink» can also mean an unpleasant, untrustworthy person.
fact: «Fink» is old-fashioned US slang, so you will mostly meet it in old films, comics and stories.
fact: Everyday words with a similar meaning are snitch, grass (UK) and tattletale (US).
exercise: «The gangster yelled, "You fink!"» What does fink mean here? | A kind doctor | *A person who tells on others | A kind of fish | A cheerful dancer
exercise: Sam told the teacher who broke the window. The other kids called him a fink. Which everyday word means the same? | Sleepy | Tidy | Happy | *Snitch
exercise: Which phrase means to tell on someone? | To dance with someone | To hide from someone | To thank someone | *To fink on someone
test: «Mia said, "Don't fink on me!"» What does fink on mean here? | *Tell on | Help | Dance | Forget
test: «You rat fink!» is said as an insult. Which best describes it? | *A mild insult | A question | A kind compliment | A greeting
exam: Which sentence uses fink as a noun? | *«The fink told the police where the money was.» | «She finks on her friends every day.» | «Let's fink on him now.»
